<h1>Pivot Conveyors: Wheel, Ball Transfer &amp; Turntable</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A pivot conveyor changes product flow direction by rotating the conveying surface or using multi-directional elements that redirect products at any angle. Pivot conveyors include pivot wheel sections, <a href="/conveyor-functions/ball-transfer-tables/">ball transfer tables</a>, and turntable pivots. These systems route products between <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor</a> lines, <a href="/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/">belt conveyors</a>, and processing stations in packaging, assembly, and distribution environments where fixed-angle transfers cannot meet routing flexibility requirements.</p>
<h2 id="pivot-wheel-heading">Pivot Wheel Sections</h2>
<p>Pivot wheel sections use rows of small polyurethane or steel wheels mounted on pivoting axle assemblies. The wheels rotate freely on their axles (providing low-friction conveying), and the axle assemblies pivot to steer product direction. Pivot wheels can be set to a fixed angle for continuous directional conveying or actuated to change angle on command for programmable routing.</p>
<h3>Fixed-Angle Pivot Wheels</h3>
<p>Fixed-angle pivot wheels are set during installation to the required routing angle (typically 30, 45, or 90 degrees). The wheels remain at that angle permanently. Products arriving on the pivot wheel section follow the wheel angle to the discharge point. Fixed-angle pivot wheels serve the same function as <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/bump-turn/">bump turns</a> but with lower friction and better small-product handling because the wheels actively guide the product rather than relying on a deflection impact.</p>
<p>Fixed pivot wheel sections handle 1-50 lbs per product. They suit light products like cartons, trays, and small cases. The wheels require no power when used in gravity or momentum-fed applications. Powered versions use a belt or drive mechanism beneath the wheels for positive propulsion.</p>
<h3>Actuated Pivot Wheels (Pop-Up Wheel Sorters)</h3>
<p>Actuated pivot wheels rise above and retract below a roller conveyor surface on command. When retracted, the rollers convey the product straight. When activated, the angled wheels pop up above the rollers and divert the product at the set angle. Pop-up wheel sorters are a high-speed divert mechanism used in <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems</a> handling 150-300 products per minute.</p>
<p>Pop-up wheel sorters handle products from 1 to 75 lbs at line speeds up to 600 FPM. Dematic, Intelligrated (Honeywell), and Bastian Solutions integrate pop-up wheel diverters into their distribution center sortation platforms. The wheels are angled at 30-45 degrees to create a gentle divert trajectory that minimizes product rotation. Multiple pop-up wheel sections along a trunk line each serve a dedicated sort lane.</p>
<h2 id="ball-transfer-heading">Ball Transfer Tables</h2>
<p>Ball transfer tables use a grid of omnidirectional ball bearings (ball transfer units) mounted flush in a flat table surface. Each ball unit contains a large load-bearing ball sitting on a hemispherical cup of smaller support balls. The load ball rotates freely in any direction, allowing products placed on the table to slide in any direction with minimal friction.</p>
<h3>Passive Ball Transfer Tables</h3>
<p>Passive ball transfer tables rely on manual pushing, gravity tilt, or external conveyors to move products across the ball field. Workers push packages across the table to reorient them, rotate them, or redirect them to different conveyor spurs. Gravity-tilted ball tables use a slight incline (1-3 degrees) to move products from a high side to a low side, with guide rails to control the path.</p>
<p>Passive ball transfer tables handle 10-500 lbs per product depending on ball unit capacity. Standard ball transfer units are rated 30-75 lbs each, and the product must span at least 3-4 balls for stable support. Heavy-duty ball units rated 150-500 lbs each handle pallet loads. Omni Metalcraft, Hytrol, and Ashland Conveyor supply ball transfer table sections and individual ball units.</p>
<h3>Pop-Up Ball Transfers</h3>
<p>Pop-up ball transfers mount between <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor</a> rollers and rise above the roller surface when activated. The balls engage the product bottom and allow multi-directional movement. External pushers, guide rails, or downstream conveyors pull the product in the desired direction across the ball field. When deactivated, the balls retract and the rollers convey the product straight.</p>
<p>Pop-up ball transfers suit applications where products must route to one of several directions from a single decision point. They handle light loads (under 75 lbs) and provide the most routing flexibility of any <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer conveyor</a> type. However, they lack positive drive, the balls reduce friction but do not propel the product. An external force must move the product across the ball field.</p>
<h3>Powered Ball Transfer Modules</h3>
<p>Powered ball transfer modules use driven balls that rotate in a controlled direction, providing positive propulsion across the table surface. Interroll and other component manufacturers produce powered ball transfer modules with individually driven balls or zones of balls driven by a common mechanism. Powered ball transfers eliminate the need for external pushers and handle higher throughput than passive ball tables.</p>
<h2 id="turntable-heading">Turntable Pivot Systems</h2>
<p>A turntable pivot rotates the entire conveyor section (and the product on it) to any desired angle. The product enters the turntable on the onboard conveyor, the turntable rotates to align with the target discharge direction, and the onboard conveyor drives the product off. Turntables handle the heaviest loads and the widest range of rotation angles of any pivot type.</p>
<h3>Case-Handling Turntables</h3>
<p>Case-handling turntables support 10-500 lbs and rotate in 2-4 seconds for 90-degree turns. The onboard conveyor is typically a short <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller</a> or belt section. A servo motor or gearmotor drives the rotation through a slewing bearing or ring gear. Position sensors and encoders provide angle feedback for precise alignment with discharge conveyors.</p>
<p>Case turntables serve applications where the product must physically rotate (barcode to a different side, label face to a scanner) and where the exit direction varies (one of 3-4 discharge paths at different angles). Dorner and mk North America offer compact turntable modules for small-product packaging lines.</p>
<h3>Pallet-Handling Turntables</h3>
<p>Pallet turntables support 2,000-10,000 lbs and handle loaded pallets. The onboard conveyor is a <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR (chain-driven live roller)</a> or chain conveyor section. The turntable rotates on a heavy-duty slewing bearing driven by a gearmotor. Rotation time is 4-8 seconds for 90 degrees, longer for 180 or 270 degrees.</p>
<p>Pallet turntables serve two functions: direction change and pallet rotation. Direction change routes the pallet to a different conveyor line. Pallet rotation orients the pallet for stretch wrapping (spinning the pallet under a wrap head), label application on a specific face, or pallet pattern orientation for automated storage. Hytrol, Intelligrated, and Dematic integrate pallet turntables into their <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet conveyor systems</a>.</p>
<h3>Continuous-Rotation Turntables</h3>
<p>Some turntable applications require continuous rotation rather than indexed positioning. Stretch wrap turntables spin the pallet continuously (8-15 RPM) while the stretch film carriage rises and lowers. Inspection turntables rotate the product slowly past fixed cameras or sensors for 360-degree surface inspection. These are not directional routing devices but product processing stations built on turntable platforms.</p>
