<h2 id="apps-heading">Pivot Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Manual workstation routing:</strong> Ball transfer tables at manual pack stations allow workers to receive products from one direction, rotate or reposition them for inspection or packing, and push them onto one of several discharge conveyors. The omnidirectional ball surface lets workers handle products freely without lifting. Ergonomic workstations in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a> use ball transfer tables between process steps.</p>
<p><strong>High-speed sortation diverting:</strong> Pop-up wheel sorters in <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems</a> divert packages from trunk lines to sort lanes at 150-300 diverts per minute. The wheels pop up, redirect the package at a 30-45 degree angle onto the sort spur, and retract before the next package arrives. Pop-up wheel sorters are a core technology in e-commerce distribution centers handling parcel and small-package sorting.</p>
<p><strong>Pallet direction change and rotation:</strong> Pallet turntables in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet handling systems</a> rotate loaded pallets for stretch wrapping, reorient pallets for label application, and route pallets between multiple conveyor lines. A turntable at a conveyor intersection serves 2-4 discharge directions from a single mechanism.</p>
<p><strong>Assembly line indexing:</strong> Turntable pivots in assembly lines rotate workpieces to present different faces to sequential assembly stations. The turntable indexes 90 or 180 degrees between stations, allowing fastening, welding, or inspection on multiple product faces without manual repositioning.</p>
<p><strong>Multi-directional <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a> release:</strong> Ball transfer sections at the end of accumulation lanes allow products to release in any direction. Products accumulated in queue release onto the ball section and get pushed or guided to the next available downstream conveyor. This provides flexible routing when downstream conditions change dynamically.</p>
<p><strong>Shipping and receiving staging:</strong> Ball transfer tables in shipping areas allow workers to slide packages from one position to another for manifesting, labeling, and loading. Packages arrive on a conveyor, transfer onto the ball table, get processed, and slide onto the outbound conveyor or directly into a truck.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Select a Pivot Conveyor</h2>
<p><strong>Routing flexibility requirements define the type.</strong> Fixed direction changes (always 90 degrees to the same lane) use pivot wheels or <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/bump-turn/">bump turns</a>. Variable direction changes (different angles, different lanes each time) use ball transfers or turntables. High-speed automated diverting uses pop-up wheel sorters.</p>
<p><strong>Load weight narrows options quickly.</strong> Under 75 lbs: any pivot type works. 75-500 lbs: ball transfer tables or case turntables. Over 500 lbs: pallet turntables with heavy-duty slewing bearings and CDLR onboard conveyors.</p>
<p><strong>Throughput drives the speed requirement.</strong> Pop-up wheel sorters handle the highest throughput (150-300 per minute). Pivot wheel sections handle continuous flow at 60-200 FPM. Ball transfer tables suit low-throughput manual operations. Turntables are the throughput bottleneck (6-15 cycles per minute for cases, 4-8 for pallets).</p>
<p><strong>Automation level sets the control complexity.</strong> Passive ball tables and fixed pivot wheels need no controls. Pop-up wheel sorters require PLC integration, barcode scanning, and real-time routing logic. Turntables need servo or VFD position control with encoder feedback. Match the control complexity to your existing <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automation infrastructure</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Product orientation behavior matters.</strong> Pivot wheels and bump turns rotate the product. Ball transfers maintain orientation (the product slides without rotating). Turntables rotate the product by the table rotation angle. Select based on whether orientation change is acceptable, desired, or prohibited for downstream operations like <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/">merging and sorting</a>.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a pivot conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A pivot conveyor changes product direction using multi-directional elements or a rotating conveyor surface. Types include pivot wheels, ball transfer tables, and turntable pivots. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer conveyor guide</a> for all direction-change methods.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the difference between a pivot wheel and a ball transfer?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Pivot wheels are angled wheels that guide products in a set direction. Ball transfers are omnidirectional ball bearings that allow movement in any direction. Pivot wheels give more directional control. Ball transfers give more routing flexibility but need external force to move the product.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">When should I use a turntable instead of a transfer?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Use a turntable when the product must physically rotate, loads exceed 500 lbs, direction angles vary, or exits are at angles other than 90 degrees. Turntables handle up to 10,000 lbs but cycle slower (6-12 seconds). Pop-up <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">chain transfers</a> are faster for fixed 90-degree routing.</p>

</div>
</div>
